{
    "repo": "cockroachdb/cockroach",
    "pr_number": 136074,
    "lang": "go",
    "pr_title": "release-24.1: upgrades: fix destructive upgrade bug that was introduced when permanent upgrades were flattened",
    "pr_body": "fixes https://github.com/cockroachdb/cockroach/issues/133519\r\nRelease justification: fixes a major bug\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n### roachtest: add test to verify that permanent upgrades are not re-executed\r\n\r\nThis test shows that there is a bug in how permanent upgrades are\r\napplied when upgrading from clusters that started on a version older\r\nthan v23.1.\r\n\r\nRelease note: None\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n### Revert \"upgrades: remove v22_2 compatibility\"\r\n\r\nThis partially reverts commit 129d62a.\r\n\r\nIt also fixes the roachtest that was added in the previous commit.\r\n\r\nIn PR #119142, the permanent upgrades were flattened so that they\r\nall have a version of 0.0-x. That means that the last element of\r\nthe permanentUpgrades slice has a version of 0.0-4. Clusters that were\r\nbootstrapped with a version from v23.1.0 or later will have rows in\r\nsystem.migrations for versions 0.0-x, since that is the version where\r\nthe permanent upgrades framework was introduced (in PR #91627).\r\nHowever, clusters that were bootstrapped from a version\r\nearlier than v23.1 will not yet have these rows in system.migrations for\r\nversions 0.0-x, and those migrations will only be present under the\r\nlegacy startupmigrations key.\r\n\r\nRelease note (bug fix): Fixed a bug that could cause the password for\r\nthe root user to be deleted while upgrading to v24.1. This bug only\r\naffects clusters that were initially created with a version of v22.2\r\nor earlier.\r\n\r\nThe same bug could also cause the `defaultdb` and `postgres` databases\r\nto be recreated during the upgrade to v24.1 if they had been previously\r\ndeleted.",
    "pr_comments": [
        "Thanks for opening a [backport](https://cockroachlabs.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/CRDB/pages/900005932/Backporting+a+change+to+a+release+branch).\n\nPlease check the backport criteria before merging:\n\n- [ ] Backports should only be created for [serious\n  issues](https://cockroachlabs.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/ENG/pages/1043235321/Database+Backport+Patch+Policy#Backport-only-for-serious-issues) or test-only changes.\n- [ ] Backports should not break backwards-compatibility.\n- [ ] Backports should change as little code as possible.\n- [ ] Backports should not change on-disk formats or node communication protocols.\n- [ ] Backports should not add new functionality (except as defined\n  [here](https://cockroachlabs.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/ENG/pages/1043235321/Database+Backport+Patch+Policy#Backporting-new-functionality-(including-modifications-to-persisted-state-%26-cross-node-RPCs))).\n- [ ] Backports **must not** add, edit, or otherwise modify cluster versions; or add version gates.\n- [ ] All backports must be reviewed by the owning areas TL. For more information as to how that review should be conducted, please consult the [backport\npolicy](https://cockroachlabs.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/ENG/pages/1043235321/Database+Backport+Patch+Policy#Getting-your-backport-reviewed).\n\n\n<details>\n  <summary>If your backport adds new functionality, please ensure that the\nfollowing additional criteria are satisfied:</summary>\n\n- [ ] There is a high priority need for the functionality that cannot wait until the next release and is difficult to address in another way.\n- [ ] The new functionality is additive-only and only runs for clusters which have specifically \u201copted in\u201d to it (e.g. by a cluster setting).\n- [ ] New code is protected by a conditional check that is trivial to verify and ensures that it only runs for opt-in clusters. State changes must be further protected such that nodes running old binaries will not be negatively impacted by the new state (with a mixed version test added).\n- [ ] The PM and TL on the team that owns the changed code have signed off that the change obeys the above rules.\n- [ ] Your backport must be accompanied by a post to the appropriate Slack\n  channel (#db-backports-point-releases or #db-backports-XX-X-release) for awareness and discussion.\n</details>\n\nAlso, please add a brief release justification to the body of your PR to justify this\nbackport.",
        "\n\n<!-- Reviewable:start -->\nThis change is\u2002[<img src=\"https://reviewable.io/review_button.svg\" height=\"34\" align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"Reviewable\"/>](https://reviewable.io/reviews/cockroachdb/cockroach/136074)\n<!-- Reviewable:end -->\n",
        "This is ready for another look.",
        "TFTR!",
        "I will forward port the test I added to later release branches once a 24.1 release with this change has been cut. (We'll want that test to use the `AlwaysUseLatestPredecessors` option.",
        "> I will forward port the test I added to later release branches\r\n\r\nHuh, my impression is that this behavior was pretty specific to the 24.1 upgrade and after 24.1 we're only using the migration keys regardless of how we got there which the exists tests cover? Is the extra test worth the non-trivial cost of a full roachtest in future versions past 24.1?",
        "Well it seems generally useful as a regression test -- we should never clear out the root password during upgrade, and there's always going to be some risk of that as long as we have code that clears out the root password during bootstrap.\r\n\r\nAlso, it will verify that this 24.1 upgrade fix really did the right thing to future-proof version upgrades that happen later on (already verified this manually, but tests with merged code seems better). For example, I could have fixed the apparent bug with different logic that just skips the root user creation in some other way, but the important thing is that this bug fix did the job of permanently tracking that the upgrade happened by writing to `system.migrations`. That's the thing that makes it so the 24.1->24.2 upgrade won't just make the same mistake.\r\n\r\n> Is the extra test worth the non-trivial cost of a full roachtest in future versions past 24.1?\r\n\r\nNon-trivial is fair, though this roachtest only uses a single node cluster, so it's cheaper than others.",
        "And it seems useful for other reasons. As far as I know, we have no other test anywhere that does a basic smoke test of upgrading CRDB from an old version like v22.1 and stepping through all the upgrades to get to the latest. We should definitely have a test like that, considering that sequence is what some of our largest and oldest customers need to do.",
        "I believe the \"version-upgrade\" roachtest does that. But it looks like it does chains of at most 4 upgrades, we could bump that up (and maybe change to the exponential distribution so in most cases we do a smaller number).",
        "> I believe the \"version-upgrade\" roachtest does that. But it looks like it does chains of at most 4 upgrades, we could bump that up (and maybe change to the exponential distribution so in most cases we do a smaller number).\r\n\r\nRight. I think that's another use-case for adding `MinimumBootstrappedVersion`. The other one we recently ran into was actually excluding `v22.2` from multi-tenant upgrades, although for now, a workaround should suffice [1].\r\n\r\n[1] https://cockroachlabs.slack.com/archives/C023S0V4YEB/p1732654970737579\r\n"
