            "> Here it is after upgrading my test cluster to v23.1.0. Note that it has a row for 0.0-4, which is also reflected in the logs I shared above.\r\n\r\nAs I mentioned, it's not always reproducable.  When I'm at 23.2, my migrations table does not have the entries for `major = 0`.  My `migrations` table is listed in the original message of this issue.\r\n\r\nMy steps are very simple:\r\n\r\n* I start with a new, empty, everything-default 3 node 21.1.0 cluster.\r\n* I replaced each binary one by one with the 21.2.0 version.  I waited until `SHOW CLUSTER SETTING version;` was `21.2`.\r\n* I replaced each binary one by one with the 22.1.0 version.  I waited until `SHOW CLUSTER SETTING version;` was `22.1`.\r\n* I replaced each binary one by one with the 22.2.0 version.  I waited until `SHOW CLUSTER SETTING version;` was `22.2`.\r\n* You can see where this is going...\r\n* This was repeated until I'm at 23.2.\r\n\r\nThen I added a brand new 4th node to the cluster of version 24.1.0.\r\n\r\nIn your test, you had `major = 0` entries after upgrading to `v23.1.0`, I do not.  I just repeated the test - no `major=0` entries:\r\n\r\n```\r\nroot@localhost:26257/defaultdb> show cluster setting version;                                               \r\n  version\r\n-----------\r\n  23.1\r\n(1 row)\r\n\r\nroot@localhost:26257/defaultdb> select * from system.migrations;                                            \r\n  major | minor | patch | internal |         completed_at\r\n--------+-------+-------+----------+--------------------------------\r\n     21 |     1 |     0 |     1104 | 2024-10-30 21:03:25.186147+00\r\n     21 |     1 |     0 |     1112 | 2024-10-30 21:03:26.971053+00\r\n     21 |     1 |     0 |     1114 | 2024-10-30 21:03:33.366245+00\r\n     21 |     1 |     0 |     1118 | 2024-10-30 21:03:34.910068+00\r\n     21 |     1 |     0 |     1120 | 2024-10-30 21:03:35.94555+00\r\n...\r\n```\r\n\r\nAnd again to v23.2.0:\r\n\r\n```\r\nroot@localhost:26257/defaultdb> show cluster setting version;                                               \r\n  version\r\n-----------\r\n  23.2\r\n(1 row)\r\n\r\nTime: 53ms total (execution 18ms / network 34ms)\r\n\r\nroot@localhost:26257/defaultdb> select * from system.migrations;                                            \r\n  major | minor | patch | internal |         completed_at\r\n--------+-------+-------+----------+--------------------------------\r\n     21 |     1 |     0 |     1104 | 2024-10-30 21:03:25.186147+00\r\n     21 |     1 |     0 |     1112 | 2024-10-30 21:03:26.971053+00\r\n     21 |     1 |     0 |     1114 | 2024-10-30 21:03:33.366245+00\r\n     21 |     1 |     0 |     1118 | 2024-10-30 21:03:34.910068+00\r\n     21 |     1 |     0 |     1120 | 2024-10-30 21:03:35.94555+00\r\n     21 |     1 |     0 |     1122 | 2024-10-30 21:03:36.935299+00\r\n     21 |     1 |     0 |     1126 | 2024-10-30 21:03:44.964079+00\r\n     21 |     1 |     0 |     1128 | 2024-10-30 21:03:47.485175+00\r\n     21 |     1 |     0 |     1130 | 2024-10-30 21:03:49.989931+00\r\n```\r\n\r\nWould the fact I'm running this on a very large or very slow cluster make a difference?",
            "Hello, would you be able to share the logs from debug.zip when you repro it?",
            "@Dedej-Bergin I've privately sent you a debug zip from my most recent test.",
            "[debug.zip](https://github.com/user-attachments/files/17779264/debug.zip)\r\n",
            "I figured out why this only reproduces sometimes. There is code here (in the 23.1 branch) that performs a stale read to check if upgrades previously have been applied: https://github.com/cockroachdb/cockroach/blob/2ee70ad63870ea3b44cb8593d0ac84d379547fc6/pkg/upgrade/upgrademanager/manager.go#L211-L236\r\n\r\nIf one goes through all the upgrades very quickly, like in a testing scenario, this stale read will say that a previous upgrade was not applied when it fact it was. Then, when it tries to apply the upgrade, a bit later on in that function, it will check if the upgrade was already done by the legacy startupmigrations framework: https://github.com/cockroachdb/cockroach/blob/2ee70ad63870ea3b44cb8593d0ac84d379547fc6/pkg/upgrade/upgrademanager/manager.go#L239-L276\r\n\r\nIf it has, the row in `system.migrations` is added. \r\n\r\nBut in a real long-running production cluster, this stale read will not return stale data, since the upgrade would have been performed long ago. So the code never gets into that second snippet above that does the work of adding in the `system.migrations` row."
        ]
    },
